<div><img loading="lazy" decoding="async" width="680" height="464" src="https://i0.wp.com/sixcolors.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/glacier-18-iphone-rotate-6c.png?fit=680%2C464&amp;ssl=1" class="attachment-medium size-medium wp-post-image" alt="Two light blue iPhones lying on their sides, stacked and overlapping, showing their frosted-glass backs with Apple logos and triple-lens camera modules, flash, and sensor. The smaller phone sits in front of the larger one." style="margin-bottom: 10px;" srcset="https://i0.wp.com/sixcolors.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/glacier-18-iphone-rotate-6c.png?w=2070&amp;ssl=1 2070w, https://i0.wp.com/sixcolors.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/glacier-18-iphone-rotate-6c.png?resize=680%2C464&amp;ssl=1 680w, https://i0.wp.com/sixcolors.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/glacier-18-iphone-rotate-6c.png?resize=1360%2C927&amp;ssl=1 1360w, https://i0.wp.com/sixcolors.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/glacier-18-iphone-rotate-6c.png?resize=768%2C524&amp;ssl=1 768w, https://i0.wp.com/sixcolors.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/glacier-18-iphone-rotate-6c.png?resize=1536%2C1047&amp;ssl=1 1536w, https://i0.wp.com/sixcolors.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/glacier-18-iphone-rotate-6c.png?resize=2048%2C1396&amp;ssl=1 2048w, https://i0.wp.com/sixcolors.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/glacier-18-iphone-rotate-6c.png?resize=690%2C470&amp;ssl=1 690w, https://i0.wp.com/sixcolors.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/glacier-18-iphone-rotate-6c.png?resize=1380%2C941&amp;ssl=1 1380w" sizes="auto, (max-width: 680px) 100vw, 680px" /></div><p>Apple doesn&#8217;t roll out new iPhones every September because it&#8217;s fond of fall colors. In fact, if you&#8217;re a fellow old like me, you might remember the days when models like the iPhone 3G or iPhone 4 hit stores in the summertime. It wasn&#8217;t until 2012 and the iPhone 5 that Apple settled on fall release dates for its main models.</p>
<p>The fall rollouts are timed so that retail shelves are packed with new models just in time for the holiday shopping season. That way, people pressed for new gift ideas can always grab the latest iPhone model, and Apple&#8217;s financial division can celebrate a merry Christmas indeed toting up the sales figures.</p>
<p>A glance at the calendar right at this moment reveals that we find ourselves in that sweet spot right between the September release of the new iPhone 18 Pro models and the time when people start stampeding to stores, online or otherwise, looking for gadgets to buy ahead of the holidays. And that means deciding whether the latest iPhones should replace whatever phone you happen to be carrying around at the moment.</p>
<p>Of course, early adopters are going to take the plunge right away. But for the rest of us, deciding whether to upgrade to a new iPhone takes time, with that go/no-go decision hinging on exactly what the latest model can do that our current phone cannot.</p>
<p>I feel like I&#8217;m well-positioned to answer that question, having handled every iPhone since the first one came out in 2007. And, more germane to the issue at hand, I&#8217;ve also had a chance to spend some time using the iPhone 18 Pro to see just what&#8217;s different with Apple&#8217;s latest phone and how it builds upon what&#8217;s come before.</p>

<h2>What&#8217;s new</h2>
<p>In case your thoughts have wandered off to more current matters like the <a href="https://sixcolors.com/post/2026/09/the-iphone-duo-tradeoffs-but-fewer-than-feared/">iPhone Duo</a> or the newly unveiled <a href="https://sixcolors.com/post/2026/10/apple-announces-homeview-smart-display/">Apple HomeView</a>, the iPhone 18 Pro debuted in September alongside the iPhone Duo as this year&#8217;s additions to the iPhone lineup. Apple only introduced Pro models alongside the iPhone Duo this fall, a break from its traditional pattern of releasing a standard iPhone flagship at the same time. Instead, you&#8217;ll have to wait for an iPhone 18 model, with rumors pointing to a spring 2027 launch.</p>
<p>The phones Apple did release this year promise better performance, longer battery life, new camera capabilities and — thanks to the inclusion of <a href="https://sixcolors.com/post/2026/09/ios-27-review-little-things-mean-a-lot/">iOS 27</a> — a smarter, more capable Siri. The phones also cost $100 more than their predecessors — the iPhone 17 Pro series — after those 2025 models saw a price hike of their own. Apple could hand-wave the higher base model cost of the iPhone 17 Pro by boosting the included storage to 256GB, so you were paying the same that you would have for a similarly sized iPhone 16 Pro. No such pricing contortions exist for the iPhone 18 Pro — these are straight-up price hikes, so you&#8217;re paying more for this year&#8217;s models than you ever have for one of Apple&#8217;s Pro phones.</p>
<p>The iPhone 18 Pro looks a lot like its predecessor, which introduced a camera plateau that stretches across the back of the phone and houses the three rear lenses. (It also frees up internal space for a bigger battery, which may be its best contribution to iPhone design.) To my eye, the colors on the iPhone 18 Pro look a little more unified than the two-tone approach of the iPhone 17 Pro that incorporated different shades.</p>
<p>I hadn&#8217;t used the iPhone 17 Pro that extensively, so holding the iPhone 18 Pro and its similar design is a bit jarring, especially when you&#8217;ve got the device in a landscape orientation. Playing a game or watching a video, I found my fingers occasionally landing on those jutting rear lenses, though that may be something I get used to with more use. Certainly, switching over from a standard iPhone 17 and its less prominent camera array, I found the Pro design quite different.</p>
<p>As for those colors, the iPhone 18 Pro comes in the standard silver and black, with glacier and burgundy options for those who want a little more flash in their lives. My glacier review model certainly looks stylish with a blueish tint to an otherwise silvery cast. Personally, though, I like brighter colors for my phone as opposed to the staid tones Apple favors for its Pro models.</p>
<p>Those are just a few minor changes introduced with the iPhone 18 Pro. The biggest additions can be summed up thusly:</p>
<p><strong>Better performance</strong>: Apple&#8217;s annual processor upgrade takes the form of the A20 Pro, a system-on-chip offering a 6-core CPU, 7-core GPU and dual 16-core Neural Engine to handle all that on-device AI that&#8217;s now front-and-center on Apple&#8217;s devices. Apple promises a boost over what the A19 Pro powering the iPhone 17 Pro models offered, but the more relevant improvement involves older phones.</p>
<p><a href="https://sixcolors.com/post/2026/10/iphone-18-pro-review-apples-priciest-pro-phone-ever-poses-a-challenge-for-upgraders/"><img src="https://mt.sixcolors.com/chart-images/43ee52ead41138435d4b65ad3380cf2f.png?s=vVLBbtQwEP0VyycQ2cRNIdlGQhw49MKKlSpOzR5mndnE1LGN7UBXq_w7kwQKFQVpkeBmv5n35r2xTxxCwBh4xbsYXaiyLKh7abX1IZW2z764lbQmoolZ7LDH8KOeLdQJWC3ISnbgCeEJbyACr07800AIen38aUIf08dDwDmNq4MyYKQCHbKJnX4M1pASGDOAPou-UBaBMeEHCkAJbx8UZiBtrW01glOLigzhzQF6pY-v3zs0L27AhOqlEEkpBN-Rjxh9mCJN5pakZGqPRnY9-LtvmadtHVRLlVPNo4oaa17VXG07a5BdlGzrLdvAPQsOsWERQ6x5Qq10CNR6S7QOoVGmnYnXiHfzDFayGwIp6FvrcebsMdJq57ZOtd10JLRBqXraAuGCrh7DoB-kDfSPDRXfDc3cz6CHqZ6vr8oxeap__WT_5VWR09V52wwyLgQ3EWo-7mah32XakDn1PyKVa5GfE-lCFIX4u0zX2w_s2QYj6Of_-p3KV_lZD1Xk4vIPqXZUMpb-4i-Z9kfSI78R2TvYh5Ta-Th-BQ" alt="iPhone 17 Pro Max speed test" width="640" style="max-width:100%;height:auto;" /></a></p>
<p>I ran Geekbench 7 on my iPhone 18 Pro and got single- and multicore scores that were impressively better than the A18 Pro-powered iPhone 16 Pro. More significantly, the iPhone 18 Pro also features a redesigned thermal management system to keep things from getting hot enough to affect performance, particularly when it comes to older models.</p>
<p>I certainly didn&#8217;t run into any problems playing PUBG Mobile, a fairly demanding multiplayer shooter game that the iPhone 18 Pro handles with aplomb. On a side note, I was able to play the game outside, and while some of the richer graphical details were lost in the sun&#8217;s glare, the action remained visible enough on the iPhone display for me to claim victory.</p>
<p><strong>Longer battery life</strong>: Apple never reveals the size of the batteries it includes in its phones but you don&#8217;t have to do much digging on the Internet to find out that the eSIM-only version of the iPhone 18 Pro sold here in the U.S. contains a 4,288 mAh power pack. (The iPhone 18 Pro with a physical SIM tray features a slightly smaller 4,056 mAh cell.) For context, the iPhone 18 Pro&#8217;s battery is just a smidge larger than what Apple used in the iPhone 17 Pro (4,252 mAh in the eSIM model) but a massive leap from the iPhone 16 Pro&#8217;s 3,582 mAh battery.</p>
<p>Apple prefers to talk about battery power in terms of how long you can perform certain tasks, with the iPhone 18 Pro capable of providing up to 45 hours of video playback. That&#8217;s a jump up from the 33 hours listed for the iPhone 17 Pro, so credit the more efficient A20 Pro chipset for that gain.</p>
<p>I certainly noticed the iPhone 18 Pro&#8217;s staying power when using the phone. One afternoon, I took the fully charged iPhone out to take photos, play some games and stream some video, wrapping up my usage by nightfall. After not plugging in the phone to recharge overnight, the iPhone 18 Pro still had 63% of its charge left the next morning. You&#8217;re getting a long-lasting phone with this model.</p>
<p><strong>Camera improvements</strong>: The triple 48MP rear cameras introduced with the iPhone 17 Pro are back for an encore with the new model, as Apple adds a new trick to the mix in the form of a variable aperture for the main lens. You can widen the aperture to f/1.48 to let in more light or opt for an f/4.0 aperture for greater depth of field. There&#8217;s also an f/2.8 setting when you want to capture more details in the background along with the f/1.8 default aperture.</p>
<p>On-board intelligence can pick the aperture, depending on what you&#8217;re shooting, how much light there is, and whether there&#8217;s motion in the shot. But in keeping with the phone&#8217;s Pro label, aperture can be set manually, too, by tapping the Camera Control button and selecting Lens Aperture.</p>
