<p>You can see the effect in this pair of shots I took of some Halloween decorations. The trees and background are much sharper in the photo with the f/4 aperture than they are in the one taken at the default setting. That&#8217;s not to say one photo is necessarily better than the other, but the iPhone 18 Pro certainly affords you more control.</p>
<figure><img data-recalc-dims="1" decoding="async" src="https://i0.wp.com/sixcolors.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/lightb-f4-6c.jpeg?ssl=1" alt="A front yard at night lit in eerie blue, filled with foam Halloween tombstones marked RIP and skulls. In the background, glowing jack-o'-lanterns sit on the steps of a house, framed by dark tree branches and foliage." /><figcaption>Image taken at f/4.</figcaption></figure>
<figure><img data-recalc-dims="1" decoding="async" src="https://i0.wp.com/sixcolors.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/lightb-143-6c.jpeg?ssl=1" alt="A front yard decorated for Halloween at night, with foam tombstones lit by blue light. Glowing jack-o'-lanterns sit on the porch steps of a house in the background, and a parked car is visible at left." /><figcaption>Image taken at f/1.48.</figcaption></figure>
<p>Toggling between apertures can change up the look of your photos. I shot an outdoor scene at night using various apertures, and the one taken at f/4 has a neat starburst effect around one of the jack-o&#8217;-lanterns in the background. That&#8217;s missing from the photo taken with an f/1.48 aperture. It&#8217;s easy to see how someone with more impressive photographic chops than mine could use the variable aperture on the iPhone 18 Pro to produce some very creative-looking images.</p>
<figure class="pull-right"><img data-recalc-dims="1" decoding="async" src="https://i0.wp.com/sixcolors.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/focus-tracking-6c-scaled.png?ssl=1" alt="iPhone camera app screenshot showing a Focus Tracking badge as a small orange-and-white cat stands by a curb near a tree trunk, fallen ginkgo leaves, and a blue flatbed truck. Zoom options and a Photo mode selector appear below." /><figcaption></figcaption></figure>
<p>You&#8217;ll find other new features with the iPhone 18 Pro&#8217;s cameras, like a smart focus tracking capability. Double-tap on whatever subject you want to track, and the iPhone 18 Pro will keep things in focus, even if that subject moves in and out of the frame. I figure this will be an excellent tool for capturing photo and video of my cat, who isn&#8217;t always the most cooperative subject when it comes to photography.</p>
<p>More advanced users will appreciate that the iPhone 18 Pro adds pro-level controls for adjusting white balance, shutter speed and more. (And thanks to a change in iOS 27, you can now pick which controls to pin to the top of the viewfinder.) The newly introduced Photographic Styles 3 lets you adjust texture and grain control alongside tone and color, while an Apple Reference Image feature offers a way of verifying a photo&#8217;s authenticity. When shooting video, you can also apply the Cinematic mode after the fact in the Photos app.</p>
<p><strong>Improved on-board intelligence:</strong> The other marquee feature for this year&#8217;s crop of iPhones is the improved artificial intelligence features highlighted by a Siri assistant that&#8217;s more aware of you and the contents of your device and can take more decisive actions. That&#8217;s something powered by iOS 27, so it&#8217;s not necessarily exclusive to the iPhone 18 Pro; any phone that supports Apple Intelligence benefits from the more advanced AI in iOS 27.</p>
<p>That said, the A20 Pro chipset and its beefier Neural Engine figure to carry out AI commands faster than on older phones. Whether that&#8217;s fast enough for the end user to notice is debatable.</p>
<p>It&#8217;s also worth noting that some iOS 27 features require newer iPhones — you&#8217;ll need an iPhone 17 or later to take advantage of the dual capture capabilities added to FaceTime, for example, while altering the expressivity and pace of Siri requires at least an iPhone 17 Pro. So if you&#8217;ve got an older device, upgrading to the iPhone 18 Pro lets you access all of iOS 27&#8217;s capabilities.</p>
<h2>Who should upgrade?</h2>
<p>People who demand the latest phone every year or join the crush of early adopters grabbing a new iPhone the moment it becomes available — I&#8217;m going to guess that Venn diagram is pretty close to a perfect circle — don&#8217;t need me to tell them if the iPhone 18 Pro merits an upgrade. Rather, let&#8217;s look at this from the perspective of someone who&#8217;s been holding on to their iPhone for at least two years and is wondering whether there are enough new features on offer to justify a purchase — particularly in light of the iPhone 18 Pro&#8217;s higher price.</p>
<p>The iPhone 18 Pro&#8217;s camera setup is definitely an upgrade for anyone with an iPhone 16 Pro or older. That particular model only had a pair of 48MP lenses on the wide and ultrawide cameras, while you made do with a 12MP sensor for telephoto shots. The iPhone 15 Pro had just a single 48MP sensor, turning to 12MP shooters for its ultrawide and telephoto cameras. You can count on higher resolution shots from the new phone even before you take the variable aperture feature into consideration.</p>
<p>And that variable aperture does give the iPhone 18 Pro&#8217;s cameras greater flexibility, even if everyone who buys the phone doesn&#8217;t end up putting the feature to extensive use. If your primary activity on the iPhone is taking photos, then an iPhone 18 Pro upgrade makes a lot of sense; if you&#8217;re more of a casual photographer, the camera upgrades are a less compelling point, impressive though they may be.</p>
<p>But the iPhone 18 Pro offers other upgrades over older phones, particularly when it comes to battery life. Apple promises nearly twice the video playback time of the iPhone 15 Pro — 45 hours on the new model compared to 23 hours with the 2023 model. iPhone 16 Pro owners can expect to get a device that lasts much longer on a charge should they upgrade.</p>
<p>Other improvements should be noticeable as well. The iPhone 18 Pro&#8217;s display is brighter than the one on either the iPhone 16 Pro or the iPhone 15 Pro. (Its 6.3-inch panel is bigger than the iPhone 15 Pro&#8217;s 6.1-inch display, too.) The Dynamic Island on the new model is smaller. And the A20 Pro powering the latest phone is more powerful than what&#8217;s come before it.</p>
<p>The fact remains, though, that you&#8217;re paying $200 more for an iPhone 18 Pro than you did for one of those earlier Pro models. That&#8217;s not chump change, especially in this economy, though you&#8217;re getting a lot of improvements to take the sting out of that higher price.</p>
<h2>What about the iPhone 18?</h2>
<p>There&#8217;s another group of would-be upgraders, though — the folks who would normally get the standard iPhone but have no iPhone 18 to turn to at this particular moment. Is the iPhone 18 Pro the model that finally convinces them to go Pro or would it be better to hold out until Apple introduces a cheaper model at some point next year?</p>
<p>I&#8217;ve been a long-time user of Apple&#8217;s standard iPhones — before the iPhone 17 I work with now, I carried around an iPhone 12. And for me, upgrading to the iPhone 18 Pro from one of those base models would be a much harder sell.</p>
<p>That&#8217;s not a reflection of the iPhone 18 Pro itself, which we&#8217;ve established as delivering a number of significant improvements. Rather, it&#8217;s the nature of the improvements — they&#8217;re fairly high-end for what someone like me uses his phone for. And I suspect that&#8217;s probably the case for owners of the iPhone 16, iPhone 15 or even earlier models.</p>
<p>As noted above, having a main camera with a variable aperture that you can control is a great feature that produces some really compelling photographs. But is that the sort of thing that moves the needle for someone used to a more basic iPhone? And even if it was more than a nice-to-have feature for that kind of iPhone owner, is it the sort of thing you&#8217;d want to pay hundreds of dollars more than what you&#8217;re used to handing over for one of Apple&#8217;s smartphones? The answer to the first question is probably not, and I&#8217;m betting the answer to the second is a &#8220;no&#8221; carved out of giant rocks.</p>
<p>No one likes waiting — Tom Petty even wrote <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=uMyCa35_mOg">a song about it</a> — but Apple is almost certainly going to release more devices into the iPhone 18 family, possibly as soon as the first few months of 2027. And when it does arrive, the iPhone 18 may not have all the Pro-level features found in the iPhone 18 Pro I&#8217;ve been testing, but it should deliver some key improvements of its own. And it certainly won&#8217;t hit the $1,199 price that Apple&#8217;s Pro phones now command.</p>
<h2>The last word</h2>
<p>Apple has set a yearly challenge for itself — release a new phone each year that&#8217;s better than the ones that came before it. The iPhone 18 Pro offers enough tweaks and new capabilities to fulfill that brief, and Pro phone users who&#8217;ve been holding on to older models will find the upgrade worthwhile, even with the higher cost. The rest of us will have to wait to see what features trickle down to the more affordable iPhone 18.</p>
